reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
CHANCE: The only randomness is the single shuffle. It decides which triples each active player sees, where the three 4-value cards per colour fall in each colour, and where the 3 Church and 3 Gold cards land. Each player sees 4 triples (12 cards), and every triple must be split three ways, so a lucky deal is diluted: one card is given to a rival and one is auctioned. I estimate roughly 25–35% of outcome variance comes from the deal.

INFERENCE: The composition is public. By the end, 24 cards are public and each player knows their own 4 kept cards, so the 8 unknown kept cards belong to the two opponents. Players can narrow these down by:
- which colour's cards are missing;
- purse jumps, which reveal kept Gold;
- dice moves when no public Church card was received, which imply a kept Church card.

PLANNING: Players must:
- decide which card to give to the rival next seat and which to expose at auction;
- budget 8 gold plus Gold cards across 12 auctions;
- value cards by marginal majority swing times the die value;
- use church votes to raise colours they lead and lower rivals' colours.

OPPONENT MODELLING: Players must predict sealed bids, use the tie-deny tactic and read talk.

WITH ALL PLAYERS PLAYING WELL: The winner depends on split choices, bidding economy and church manipulation applied to a shared, mostly inferable state, not on a hidden type alone. A strong reasoner is clearly favoured over a careless bidder or splitter.
